a new Pentagon report describes what  happened during an ambush in the air  that killed four American soldiers and  injured two others  the report says multiple failures  including a lack of proper training led  to the four men's deaths the classified  version of the full report has yet to be  released  David Martin is at the Pentagon with new  information on that October firefight  David good morning good morning the  investigation found that a series of  failures by people and organizations  left that Patrol unable to deal with an  ambush they never saw coming  they were outnumbered three to one by a  well-trained and well-armed enemy if you  get to a position on in an operation  where you're under enemy contact you  need to be able to operate like  clockwork without having to speak  because you know the drills and in this  particular case the team did not conduct  those basic soldier level skills general  thomas waldhauser commander of US troops  in africa said the team of eleven  americans had not trained together  enough then they set out to capture a  terrorist a mission for which they did  not have proper approval but lead  investigator Major General Roger  Cloutier said the mistakes should not  obscure one other fact there were  numerous acts of extraordinary bravery  that occurred on that day the military's  digital recreation shows that when the  ambush hit the troops returned fire but  soon realized they were outgunned and  outnumbered and tried to drive out of  the kill zone one vehicle was left  behind sergeant Brian Black was the  first to fall sergeants Jeremiah Johnson  and Dustin Wright stayed with him until  overwhelming enemy fire forced them to  run  sergeant Jeremiah Johnson was shot and  rendered immobile sergeant Wright then  stopped to return to his teammates  position continuing to engage the enemy  until both were fatally wounded the rest  moved to a second position were again  overwhelmed and had to retreat unable to  make it to his vehicle sergeant Lee  David Johnson made a nearly eleven  hundred yard dash for Life which ended  at this thorn tree he continued to fight  despite early reports he had been  captured  Sarla David Johnson first of all died  act  engaging the enemy he was never captured  alive his hands were never bound 800  Americans remained in Niger but they  have now been given armoured vehicles in  which to patrol and told to scale back  their operations in order to reduce the  chances of coming in contact with the  enemy  John David thanks so much so now we know  what happened with the question why  there are those multiple failures and  John here that the family has to here  lack of proper training and a series of  failures led to the death of your loved  one that you get to see how it played  out it's got to be very painful  absolutely
 

